Ordering behavior
Data frames are published immediately when received by the FEC decoder. Recovered frames are published when parity plus exactly k-1 data frames are available. There is no sequence-order holdback in receiver.cpp; publish_frame is called by decoder.receive and then dedupe decides whether the sequence is still admissible. A late recovered frame can be rejected as too_old if the dedupe base already advanced past it.

Timeout risk
When a generation fills to k, parity is emitted on close_by_k and the timeout is not on the critical path. When a partial generation does not reach k, parity is emitted only after --fec-timeout-us expires from the first frame in that generation, or at final flush. Therefore a loss inside a partial generation can add up to the configured timeout before recovery is even possible, plus scheduling, send, receive, and decode time. The current default is 200 us.

What needs to be measured before final claims
Add fec_recovery_p999_ns or export raw recovery latencies.
Run controlled EC2 two-host tests with injected single loss in partial generations and full generations separately.
Report recovery latency distributions split by close reason: closed_by_k, closed_by_timeout, closed_by_flush.
Report whether recovered frames were published, rejected too_old, or superseded by later window advancement.
